Now about age. I'm leaving it out of the scoring, and I think your boss should hear why. In most places, including the UK and the US, scoring people by age is illegal discrimination. It's also a bad business decision. People retire at a fixed age, so age tells you almost nothing about how long someone will stay in the role. Plenty of research shows that older workers are just as productive as younger ones.

What I can't do is weight applicants down simply because they have gaps in their employment. Gaps are common and often have ordinary reasons: illness, caring for someone, redundancy, a long job search, raising children. They tend to hit women and people from lower-income backgrounds hardest, which means a gap weight works as a proxy for sex and class even if that isn't your intention. It also punishes the very people who need jobs most. If your boss wants to measure reliability, look at time in each role, which the function already rewards through years of experience.

If the postcode field is meant to stand in for income or area, I'd drop it too. It points in the same direction as age and gaps, and the same concerns apply.

